      "id": 1,
      "text": "Seacliff State Beach was one of California’s first state beaches, opening in 1931.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seacliff%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The historic SS Palo Alto, a concrete tanker from WWI, now sits on Seacliff Beach as a marine haven for sea life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.santacruz.org/blog/beachside-bliss-seacliff/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Seacliff’s name comes from its seaside location, and the area was once part of Rancho Aptos before becoming a bustling shipping port.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seacliff%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the 1850s Thomas Fallon turned part of Rancho Soquel into the resort New Brighton, which borders Seacliff’s mile‑long beach.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=29738",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Ohlone Indians thrived here for thousands of years, harvesting fish, shellfish, and edible plants from the rich coastal resources.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=29738",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
